Q: 2 flat arrays (keys/values) to nested JSON A: <p>My approach uses <code>zip()</code> to map keys to values. It assumes that a key appears only once within a subgroup, allowing grouping to be performed using <code>defaultdict</code> and <code>dict</code>. The complete graph is then constructed from this structure.</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>from collections import defaultdict
import json 

keys = ['a', 'b', 'c', 'a', 'b', 'c', 'a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'a', 'b', 'c', 'd']
values = ['12', '9', '4', '5', '14', '6', '3', '3', '10', '9', '4', '6', '11', '4']

children = defaultdict(dict) 
cnt = 1
for k,v in zip(keys, values):
    if k in children[cnt]:
        cnt += 1
    children[cnt][k] = int(v)

graph = {
    'name': 'graph', 
    'children': [
        {
            'name': k, 
            'value': sum(v.values()), 
            'children': [{'name': k_, 'value': v_} for k_,v_ in v.items()]
        } for k,v in children.items()
    ]
}

print(json.dumps(graph, indent=4))
</code></pre>

Q: How do I remove a style element from an inline style using regex? A: <p>Since your style is in a DOM element with explicit ID &quot;first_line_info&quot;, the simplest way to achieve your goal would be:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script&gt;
  // Step 1: Get the element by its ID
  var element = document.getElementById(&quot;first_line_info&quot;);

  // Step 2: Remove the float style
  element.style.float = null;

&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>If you want to make such code a function so that it can also apply to other elements, you can create a function, like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script&gt;
  function removeFloatStyleById(elementId) {
    // Make sure the function has an element ID, which should always be a string
    if (elementId === undefined) {
      console.error(&quot;removeFloatStyleById requires an element ID argument.&quot;);
      return;
    }
    if (typeof elementId !== &quot;string&quot; || elementId.trim() === &quot;&quot;) {
      console.error(&quot;removeFloatStyleById requires a non-empty string ID.&quot;);
      return;
    }
    var element = document.getElementById(elementId);
    if (element) {
      element.style.float = null;
    } else { // Comment out this section if you don't care if the ID is unmatched
      console.warn(&quot;removeFloatStyleById - No element found with ID:&quot;, elementId);
    }
  }
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>... So that elsewhere you can use removeFloatStyleById(&quot;ID-of-the-elemenet&quot;) on the elements.<br />
As @vusan kindly noted and provided his answer previously, using DOM manipulation is a preferred way than Regex when CSS/style is involved.<br />
But according to your comment in question, it seems you wanted to remove the &quot;float&quot; property from not just multiple elements but all elements in the entire document. If so, here's a version for it (no function needed):</p>
<pre><code>&lt;script&gt;
  // Remove &quot;float&quot; style from all DOM elements.
  // Step 1: Select all elements in the document
  var elements = document.querySelectorAll(&quot;*&quot;);
  // Step 2: Loop through and check if they have inline float
  elements.forEach(function(element) {
    if (element.style &amp;&amp; element.style.float) {
      element.style.float = null;
    }
  });
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: How do I scope a VS Code custom snippet to files with &quot;.sh&quot; or &quot;.bash&quot; filename extensions? A: <p>VSCode snippets now have <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/editing/userdefinedsnippets#_file-pattern-scope" rel="nofollow noreferrer">File Pattern Scope</a>, allowing you to specify glob patterns for files that match/don't match the patterns.</p>
<ul>
<li><code>&quot;include&quot;</code> : Only provide snippet in files matching this pattern</li>
<li><code>&quot;exclude&quot;</code> : Do not provide snippet in files matching this pattern</li>
</ul>
<p>You can use both to create a broad <code>include</code> and exclude more specific files.</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;cheer&quot;:{
    &quot;prefix&quot;: &quot;viva&quot;,
    &quot;scope&quot;: &quot;markdown&quot;,
    &quot;include&quot;: &quot;**/emails/*&quot;,
    &quot;exclude&quot;: &quot;*toCops*&quot;,
    &quot;body&quot;: [
      &quot;We Attack At Midnight.&quot;
      &quot;Viva La Revolution!&quot;,
    ],
    &quot;description&quot;: &quot;&quot;
  },
  &quot;Snitch&quot;:{
    &quot;prefix&quot;: &quot;snitch&quot;,
    &quot;scope&quot;: &quot;markdown&quot;,
    &quot;include&quot;: &quot;**/emails/*&quot;,
    &quot;exclude&quot;: &quot;*toAccomplices*&quot;,
    &quot;body&quot;: [
      &quot;This was all $1's idea. I'm totally innocent.&quot;
    ],
    &quot;description&quot;: &quot;&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Issue with a () ~&gt; bool function in Dafny A: <p>Fixed. It it was just an affair of using a wrapper</p>
<pre><code>type Runnable = Function&lt;(),()&gt;
type Predicate = Supplier&lt;bool&gt;
type Supplier&lt;OutType&gt; = Function&lt;(),OutType&gt;
type Consumer&lt;InType&gt; = Function&lt;InType,()&gt;
trait Function&lt;InType,OutType&gt; {
  method runWith(input : InType) returns (output : OutType)
}
</code></pre>
